    <solution><p>A horizontal tangent line occurs where the derivative is <m>0\text{.}</m></p>
<p><me>\begin{aligned}
          f'(x) \amp = 2ax+b\\
          0 \amp = 2ax+b\\
          x \amp = -\frac{b}{2a}.
          \end{aligned}</me></p>
<p>Substituting this into <m>f(x)</m> gives</p>
<p><me>\begin{aligned}
          y \amp = a\left(-\frac{b}{2a}\right)^2+b\left(-\frac{b}{2a}\right)+c\\
          \amp = c-\frac{b^2}{4a}.
          \end{aligned}</me></p>
<p>Therefore, the point is <m>\left(-\frac{b}{2a}, c-\frac{b^2}{4a}\right)\text{.}</m></p>
